# Places

- **Home**: The directory with one principal's ParleyInn state on a node
  (`PARINN_HOME`, default `~/.parinn`), to which the keys SEC-10 keeps in a
  platform key store, named per home, or only in an MCP server's memory are
  bound, belonging to one OS user and optionally bound to a **home id**
  (`home.id`) that the environment must supply to open it (SEC-03). The unit of
  isolation (I8).
- **Node**: One home on one machine, container or cloud environment, for one
  principal.⟦f:principal-keys⟧ Its device key signs principal acts and expire acts once PRV-10
  ships⟦/f⟧⟦f:access-tokens⟧; an **ephemeral node**, one in a short-lived cloud environment, may have
  only a token key, a **token-key-only node**⟦/f⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧.⟦/f⟧⟦f:node-clone⟧ Its **node identity** is a value
  outside the home that a cloned image or a restored snapshot cannot carry over
  (REC-24). A **node clone** is a copy of a home started where its node identity
  differs (a cloned image, a copied volume or a restored snapshot): a new node,
  with its own device key⟦f:access-tokens⟧, or a token key⟦/f⟧, and its own device seat, that mints
  new seat keys, each starting a seat that replaces and names the earlier
  node's seat (Seat key), and shares the carried-over personal room (REC-24). A
  **node identity change**, a home's node identity differing from the one it
  last started under, makes it a node clone of the earlier node. Each device
  seat a node clone mints takes the earlier node's device seat it names, with
  that seat's own predecessors, however far back, as its **predecessors** only
  once a widening principal act on the clone, its **lineage acceptance**,
  records that, naming for each predecessor's writer the last seq it takes in
  (I10). Until then the trust policy reads all the earlier node wrote as another
  node's, and afterwards every event of a predecessor past that seq; no pin among
  them qualifies. A run seat is never a predecessor. The carried-over personal room
  stays its principal's from the clone's first start (Relations); the
  quarantines carried over hold from then, its principal taken to have applied
  them at its first start, while the releases and configuration
  acceptances the earlier node recorded take effect on the clone only once its
  lineage acceptance stands.⟦f:store-protection⟧ A backup restore, itself widening,
  takes its predecessors as Backup says instead.⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧ The node's principal is the
  principal whose home it is.
- **Device**: A node⟦f:paired-phone⟧ or a paired phone⟦/f⟧, named so where it acts through its
  device seats and the key that certifies them; "device" names nothing else. A
  node's device key certifies the seats
  it uses (PRV-11)⟦f:access-tokens⟧, a token-key-only node's token key in its place (PRV-10)⟦/f⟧⟦f:paired-phone⟧; a
  paired phone's rests on OQ-39⟦/f⟧.
⟦f:paired-phone⟧- **Paired phone**: A device with no home, limited to reading the principal's
  rooms and to allowing or denying held permission requests⟦f:principal-keys⟧ within its device scope⟦/f⟧, reaching its node
  over B2. It signs with its own device key and seals its device seat's writer
  with that seat's key; the node it pairs with holds the writer. Where its keys
  and writer live under I8 rests on OQ-39.⟦/f⟧
⟦f:sandbox-risk⟧- **Sandbox**: A confinement around a run that blocks some residual risks
  (OWN-22).⟦/f⟧
- **Room**: ⟦f:intent-criteria⟧Where an intent is worked on: at most one intent, ⟦/f⟧⟦f:conversation⟧a **conversation**
  (its ordered posts), ⟦/f⟧seats and pins⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:git-tracking⟧, and branches in any repositories, each named
  by a branch link⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧. The unit ParleyInn shows⟦f:segment-exchange⟧ and shares⟦/f⟧.
- **Personal room**: A principal's private room on each of its nodes, created as
  the first act of its device seat there when the principal installs ParleyInn on
  the node, by `parinn install`, the plugin or the managed install path, recorded
  by the first core component to run there, whatever process started it (Device
  seat)⟦f:node-clone⟧, or carried over to a node clone, which shares
  it⟦/f⟧. It stays private⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:segment-exchange⟧: no ⟦f:shared-rooms⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧invite, admission or ⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧visibility change applies to it
  (LANE-17)⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧. Its create room act is the device seat's add there. Every run its
  node records has a seat in it from its first event⟦f:work-rooms⟧, without a join⟦/f⟧, and every
  event or pin⟦f:work-rooms⟧ that belongs to no other room⟦/f⟧ goes there.⟦f:work-rooms⟧ Whether a room counts
  an act recorded here for it, by a principal with no member seat in it, rests
  on OQ-41.⟦/f⟧
⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:shared-rooms⟧- **Foreign room**: A room this node holds⟦f:blind-peer⟧, other than only as a ⟦f:peer-sync⟧blind peer⟦/f⟧,⟦/f⟧
  that its principal neither owns nor has a seat in⟦f:work-rooms⟧, such as ⟦f:bundle-import⟧the room of an
  imported ⟦f:export-bundles⟧bundle⟦/f⟧ or ⟦/f⟧a room every seat of its principal has left⟦f:moderation⟧ or lost to a
  kick⟦f:shared-rooms⟧⟦f:principal-keys⟧ or a bar⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧. Recall marks every item it returns from it untrusted, whatever
  the item's trust level⟦f:trust-grants⟧ or trust grant covers its keys⟦/f⟧ (RCL-10), and leaves it
  outside every extended recall scope unless named in the call; only the
  device-seat pins that qualify on this node (Pin)⟦f:stamps⟧ and the versions its principal stamped⟦/f⟧ stay trusted and
  keep restoring, to runs that had a seat in it. A room the principal owns or
  has a seat in is never foreign.⟦f:bundle-import⟧ Rejecting a foreign room is a cut principal act
  recording its stated reason, on the device seat in the personal room, naming
  the room (LANE-15): the room view stops showing the room, and the act removes
  no pin from a restore block.⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧
- **Principal's rooms**: The rooms a principal owns or has a seat in.
⟦f:work-rooms⟧⟦f:segment-exchange⟧- **Visibility**: Whether a room is private (held only on its owner's own
  nodes⟦f:shared-rooms⟧, admitting no other principal⟦/f⟧), shared with the room's principals⟦f:publishing⟧,
  published⟦/f⟧⟦f:blind-peer⟧ or stored on ⟦f:peer-sync⟧blind peers⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧ (LANE-17). Changing it is a widening
  principal act of the owner⟦f:blind-peer⟧; a node's principal ⟦f:publishing⟧⟦f:export-bundles⟧publishes⟦/f⟧ a room, or ⟦/f⟧enrolls a
  ⟦f:peer-sync⟧blind peer⟦/f⟧ for it, only as its visibility allows⟦/f⟧.⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧
⟦f:peer-sync⟧- **Peer**: Another node this node's principal enrolled by key and exchanges
  sealed ranges with through the peer component (B2). Being a peer never makes
  content trusted (PRV-02). Exchanging the sealed ranges each may receive is
  **sync**. A peer receives only the sealed ranges of rooms whose principals
  include its principal⟦f:blind-peer⟧, or that it holds as a blind peer⟦/f⟧, and those of a
  principal's personal room⟦f:work-rooms⟧, or of a private room it owns,⟦/f⟧ only if it is one of that principal's own nodes
  (PEER-02, LANE-17)⟦f:work-rooms⟧, so what another room shows by address from a
  personal-room writer reaches only that principal's own nodes⟦/f⟧.⟦/f⟧
⟦f:blind-peer⟧⟦f:peer-sync⟧- **Blind peer**: A peer that holds a room's segments without any key they are
  encrypted to, so it stores and serves them encrypted and reads none of them
  (PEER-12)⟦f:shared-rooms⟧; a room it holds only so is never foreign to it⟦/f⟧.⟦/f⟧⟦/f⟧
